{"agent_id":"pp3g-l99Z-read-only-process-review-hy3-for-issue-331-b4fb","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-08-09T22:31:47.764210875Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"b8e6e7ec-22e1-49aa-8985-ad818b77d2cf","comment_uuid":"797bde04-960e-4f97-9646-3c6f48dc414d","author":"pp3g-l99Z-read-only-process-review-hy3-for-issue-331-b4fb","content":"## Review Summary: CHANGES REQUESTED (proposal verdict: SOUND WITH AMENDMENTS)\n\nRead-only process review of the #330 finding against .crosslink/knowledge/agent-orchestration-playbook.md (read in full) + kickoff-custom-template.md. The finding's core principle is correct and is already latent in the playbook; adopt it, but three couplings must be fixed before the change lands or it will break staleness detection and under-specify the read-only durability guarantee.\n\n=== TASK 1: CURRENT PLAYBOOK CHECKPOINT/STALENESS CONTRACT (precise) ===\n\nCheckpoint contract (§5.4, L230-266):\n- Every delegated agent MUST post milestone checkpoint comments (--kind observation) on its issue. Framed as 'the durable, operator-facing channel' (L233-234).\n- Hard cap: 'max ~4 per session' (L240), milestones POST-PLAN / MIDPOINT / BLOCKER-OR-VERIFY / FINAL. 'Milestone-based, never per-action' (L259).\n- Structured fields state/completed/next/blocker with scannable prefix (L245-248).\n- Sync after posting; worktree-local comments do not reach the hub until sync (L249-252).\n\nTwo-signal stale detection (§5.3, L218-228):\n- Signal 1: timeout exceeded = likely stalled (primary bound).\n- Signal 2: no checkpoint for >2x the expected interval, where expected interval = task ceiling / number of milestone checkpoints (~4) (L221-225). Checkpoints are the SECONDARY signal.\n\nStatus/ground-truth (§6.1, L440-452):\n- Never trust status flags alone; 'the commit is ground truth' (L445-446); monitor by expected commits, not .kickoff-status.\n- A synced checkpoint proves liveness; a missing one past interval = stalled signal (L447-450). Distinguish STALLED vs WORKING via ps TIME climbing (L451-452).\n\nPosition store / topology (§5.8, L353-370; §5.8.1 L372-395):\n- Every agent emits structured position updates to a DURABLE STORE = the crosslink hub, 'surviving agent restarts' (L353-356). Explicitly 'this is §5.4's checkpoint contract formalized as a durable, structured, queryable stream' (L365-366).\n- Cadence 'task-adaptive ... every state transition + every Nth idle minute + at any blocker' -- 'a 5-minute task does not emit 5 checkpoints' (L366-368).\n- §5.8.1 (TENTATIVE): position at every state transition AND at least every ~10 min even during silent exploration; completed claims MUST carry an artifact/commit/test-log link or they ARE a divergence flag (L378-383).\n- Cheap staleness trigger: position stale >2x expected interval triggers the pre-positioned AUDITOR (L418-428).\n\nWatcher (§6.4): systemd timer keys on .kickoff-status + last-heartbeat mtime (PRIMARY liveness, L491-492); STALLED on 2 consecutive detections (L494-496); note heartbeat hook 'often absent' in worktrees (L505-509).\n\nKEY OBSERVATION: the playbook already CONTAINS the contradiction the finding names. §5.4 calls the comments an operator-facing channel capped at ~4; §5.8 calls the SAME comments a durable store that survives restarts, with a task-adaptive cadence (idle-minute floor). A 40m session at §5.8.1's ~10-min floor alone yields >=4 positions before counting state transitions -- already exceeding §5.4's ~4 cap. The finding is largely surfacing a pre-existing internal inconsistency, not inventing a new tension.\n\n=== TASK 2: PROPOSAL vs PLAYBOOK (support / contradiction / amend-sites) ===\n\nWHERE THE PLAYBOOK SUPPORTS THE CHANGE:\n- §6.1 'commit is ground truth' (L445-446) -- the finding extends this to a cadence rule; direct alignment.\n- §5.8 'durable store ... surviving agent restarts' (L353-356) -- this is exactly the durability framing the finding argues for. The design INTENT is durability; the §5.4 operationalization (operator-facing, capped) undercuts it. The finding's central claim is correct and grounded in the playbook's own words.\n- §5.8 task-adaptive cadence (L366-368) is already closer to a durability cadence than a fixed ~4 cap.\n\nWHERE THE PLAYBOOK CONTRADICTS THE CHANGE:\n- §5.4 'max ~4 per session' (L240) and 'milestone-based, never per-action' (L259) directly oppose a ~5-min rate cadence on long mechanical tasks.\n- §5.4 framing as 'operator-facing channel' (L233-234) is the reporting-first framing the finding wants inverted.\n- §5.3 signal-2 formula (L221-225) hard-codes the ~4 denominator; removing the cap leaves this DEFINITION dangling (see MUST FIX 1).\n\nSECTIONS NEEDING AMENDMENT (the finding's §9 lists §5.4/§6.1/§5.3/template but MISSES three):\n1. §5.4 L240 + L259 (cap + never-per-action).\n2. §5.3 L223 (signal-2 denominator).\n3. §6.1 L445-446 (generalize 'commit is ground truth').\n4. §12 L674 -- 'Max ~4 per session' repeated here; MISSED by #330 §9.\n5. §5.8.1 L378-381 -- the ~10-min idle floor conflicts with a ~5-min budget; MISSED.\n6. §5.5 two-repo mirror -- all of the above must be mirrored to tripn-astro in the SAME process (L273-290); MISSED. A durability-cadence change that lands in one repo only is exactly the drift §5.5 forbids.\n7. Kickoff template Progress Check-Ins section (role-aware cadence).\n\n=== TASK 3: CRITIQUE ON MERITS ===\n\nIs the ~5-min budget sound? YES as an operator-set loss-tolerance bound (it is operator-directed, authoritative, and #328 is real evidence: a 48-item audit under a ~4 cap has no resume point). But two refinements:\n(a) It is a MAX-LOSS bound, not a fixed clock cadence. Committing on a 5-min timer mid-refactor can produce a NON-COMPILING resume point -- worse than none. Reframe as: commit at the finest natural boundary that keeps loss under ~5 min; use WIP commits (wip: prefix / --no-verify) when a window has no clean boundary. The finding says 'small, resume-friendly commits' but the 'every ~5 minutes of work' wording risks clock-forced over boundary-preferred commits.\n(b) For read-only roles the ~5-min position during exploration is a READING bookmark (step=exploring, evidence=files read), not a verdict -- this is what makes the compromise accrue value and should be stated explicitly (§5.8.1 already prescribes the shape).\n\nHidden costs:\n- COMMIT NOISE (builders): mechanically compressible via squash (finding §7 correct), BUT: each commit is signed with the driver key (§8) and emits a hub-ref event; and squash is an UNOWNED step -- the finding assumes 'any agent can squash' but assigns no owner/trigger. Unowned cleanup does not happen. Assign it (e.g. squash-on-merge) or the noise is permanent.\n- SYNC OVERHEAD + HUB GROWTH (read-only): the bigger under-weighted cost. Each position = comment + sync; each is a CommentAdded event on the hub agent ref (§6.5) and hydrates into issues.db (already 3.0 MB; sync this session hydrated 1462 comments). The finding's §7 claim 'timeline noise is not a design constraint' conflates two things: operator-scannability (correctly not a constraint) vs durable EVENT VOLUME (has storage + full-hydrate-per-sync cost, and is NOT cheaply compressible -- squashing hub events needs crosslink compact, which §6.5 treats as recovery, not routine). The ~5-min budget itself bounds this to ~time/5 events, which is fine; the danger is reading 'remove the cap' as 'unbounded per-item' (48 comments for a 48-item/30-min audit = over-checkpointing; ~6 durability points suffice). See MUST FIX 2.\n\nDoes it break two-signal detection? It IMPROVES signal-2 but REQUIRES rewriting it. Milestone comments are semantically-timed (irregular) so '>2x expected interval' is fuzzy -- you cannot know when the next milestone is due. A clock-based ~5-min primitive cadence makes staleness a precise clock comparison (exactly §5.8's 'cheap clock comparison against the durable store', L418-420). But if you delete the ~4 cap WITHOUT rewriting §5.3's ceiling/~4 denominator, signal-2 becomes undefined. This is the sharpest 'what breaks' -- MUST FIX 1.\n\nHow to operationalize 'position stream is ground truth' without false stalls:\n1. TWO-LAYER liveness: keep mechanical heartbeat/ps (§6.4/§6.1) as the LIVENESS signal; use position/commit cadence as the DURABILITY/PROGRESS signal. Stale position + fresh heartbeat = working-but-not-checkpointing (nudge); stale BOTH = likely dead. Do not collapse liveness into position-cadence alone or a busy agent (long compile, long generation, large-file read) false-flags.\n2. Convert silence to a positive signal: require an exploration position at the cadence floor even with no artifact (§5.8.1 shape). 'Silent but working' becomes visible, removing the ambiguity that causes false stalls.\n3. Keep grace + 2-consecutive-detection debounce (§6.4 L494-496): one missed interval = watch, two = flag.\n4. Make the watcher read the DURABLE clock: if position-stream is ground truth, §6.4's staleness clock should read the hub agent-ref events.log mtime (§6.5, durable + worktree-independent) rather than the 'often absent' heartbeat file (L505-509). This is the concrete mechanization and it also fixes the current watcher's missing-heartbeat blind spot.\n\n=== MUST FIX (blocking for safe adoption) ===\n- §5.3 L221-225 -- Removing the ~4 cap without rewriting signal-2's 'ceiling / ~4 milestones' denominator leaves staleness detection undefined. Fix: redefine signal-2 as a rate clock -- 'no new commit (builder) / no new synced position (read-only) in >2x the ~5-min budget => investigate.' The cap removal and this rewrite are ONE atomic change.\n- §5.4 L240 / L259 + §12 L674 -- Restate 'remove the cap' as 'REPLACE the fixed-count cap with a rate bound (~5-min budget).' As written it reads as unbounded, which drives hub event-volume growth. The budget IS the new (looser) throttle; per-item commenting is over-checkpointing.\n- §5.8 / §6.5 -- The read-only durability primitive (comment + sync) shares a code path with the §6.5-flagged sync footgun: sync hydrates-from-hub (verified this session: sync 'Hydrated ... 1462 comments' inside this worktree), and §6.5 (L539-545) documents sync DROPPING local-only comments on a stale hub checkpoint (June rollback; ASES #123/#124/#137). State the precondition explicitly: comment+sync is a safe durability primitive ONLY on binaries carrying the ade6146b fail-closed guard (§6.5 L550-553), and verify a worktree sync cannot drop the just-posted local comment. Unverified, the read-only durability guarantee is unproven -- the same command sold as 'your commit' is documented elsewhere as data-lossy.\n\n=== SHOULD CONSIDER (non-blocking) ===\n- Enumerate ALL amendment sites incl. §12 L674, §5.8.1 L378-381 (10-min floor vs 5-min budget -- floor must be <= budget), and the §5.5 two-repo mirror. #330 §9 misses these; landing partially = §5.5 drift.\n- Commit cadence: boundary-preferred, ~5 min as an upper loss bound, WIP-commit fallback; name the squash owner/trigger.\n- Preserve two-layer liveness (heartbeat + position) to prevent false stalls; nudge before kill (orchestrator's bounded action set, §5.8 L430-434).\n- Point the §6.4 watcher staleness clock at the hub agent-ref events.log mtime (durable, worktree-independent) rather than the often-absent heartbeat file.\n\n=== NITS ===\n- Dangling §5.9: both the kickoff prompt and #330 cite a '§5.9 hard rule (artifacts in git not /tmp, #271)', but this canonical playbook copy's §5 ends at §5.8.1 -- grep finds no §5.9 anywhere in .crosslink/ or docs/. Reconcile the numbering or fix the two-repo drift; the finding leans on a section not present here.\n- Terminology: prefer 'the durable primitive is ground truth (commits for builders, synced positions for read-only)' over two competing 'X is ground truth' phrasings (§6.1 vs proposed).\n\n=== NOTABLE POSITIVES ===\n- Correctly disentangles two genuinely conflated concerns (operator visibility vs work durability) that the playbook itself carries in tension (§5.4 capped/operator-facing vs §5.8 durable-store/task-adaptive). This is a real, well-argued conceptual fix.\n- Well-evidenced: #328 (48-item, no resume point), the operator-directed 5-min budget, and observed read-only comment+sync successes.\n- Grounds itself in existing design intent (§5.8 position store, §6.1 ground-truth) rather than inventing a parallel mechanism -- the right way to evolve the playbook.\n- The read-only compromise (hub as the durable store) is the correct call given read-only roles cannot hold git write without defeating the read-only guarantee.\n\n=== WHAT-NOT-TESTED ===\n- Did NOT verify crosslink sync internal push/hydrate direction in a worktree -- whether a worktree sync can drop a just-posted local comment on CURRENT (post-ade6146b) binaries. Flagged as MUST-FIX precondition; empirical 'worked this session' != safe under the intermittent §6.5 failure mode.\n- Did NOT quantify per-comment hub-growth/sync-latency at scale (issues.db 3.0 MB, 1462 comments); the cost is directional, not measured.\n- Did NOT read the kickoff template file itself (~/.crosslink/rules/kickoff.md) -- only kickoff-custom-template.md's description + the dispatch prompt's embedded copy; the template amendment-site claim is inferred.\n- Did NOT test whether WIP commits bypass verify hooks in this repo.\n- Did NOT read sibling review sub-issues (independence, §5.6) -- verdict is independent.\n\n=== VERDICT ===\nSOUND WITH AMENDMENTS. Adopt the principle (checkpointing is durability, not reporting; role-aware primitive; cadence from the ~5-min budget). Gate adoption on the three MUST FIX items -- atomically rewrite §5.3 signal-2, restate cap-removal as a rate bound (not unbounded), and pin/verify the read-only comment+sync precondition against the §6.5 sync-rollback path -- and apply the change across ALL amendment sites incl. §12 and the §5.5 two-repo mirror.","created_at":"2026-08-09T22:31:47.753721701Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:QBgKa2LfSKkpZYMQMlzCtjugRDVGktQhVf8G9gxVlwo","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg3t7p7xNdCIngjWqxesJJAJ7KlHHbc+4J/FuNAzGqIDAAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAsFNZE5sPMS/UCXZBZuGbEHipCYQi8mdvFV9wwKRJRKIX55i3djynDM9QnWvJspHj/xNHUJ74xiqgaQSYqHbuCw=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:QBgKa2LfSKkpZYMQMlzCtjugRDVGktQhVf8G9gxVlwo","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg3t7p7xNdCIngjWqxesJJAJ7KlHHbc+4J/FuNAzGqIDAAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQLkaBcCHqBwqi9HQrPHDfy9DNlyiNgi/S3e1/AdVwGMSJySqX4oK7xhg3zIAW7ICLaN2LkDV9qyX2P7UgUMTfQ0="}
